title: The Reading — if Piotr was right
~~~~

Omer: Okay. So. I sent it to twelve. Eight made something. Two made something I would put on a wall.

Piotr: Show me. [face=driven]

Omer: *(sharing screen)* This one is from a student who works at — uh — at the diner near campus. Look at where she put "labor law."

Piotr: *(after a beat)* That's good. [face=smile]

John: That is — actually, that is well-organized. Did you give her that structure?

Omer: I gave her a starter line with two boxes. She added the other eleven herself.

John: Hm.

Piotr: John.

John: I am looking at it.

Piotr: John, I want you to say the words.

John: I am not going to say the words.

Omer: *(quietly, scrolling)* The next one is from a student who didn't speak in class for the first six weeks of the semester.

Piotr: *(reading)* ...She mapped her grandmother's diabetes to four disciplines.

Omer: She mapped her grandmother's diabetes to four disciplines and then she wrote three sentences underneath about why two of them were the same fight in different uniforms.

--- pause ---

John: I would like to revise — uh — not retract, but revise — one of my earlier —

Piotr: *(immediately)* Send me what they made.

John: I literally just said —

Piotr: I want them on the wall in my office. The actual diagrams. Print them.

Omer: *(smiling)* I will send them. To both of you. [face=smile]

John: ...Yes. Please.

Omer: And — for the record. I want to say something. None of this works without all three of us in the room. Including the part where you two yell at each other. Because the yelling is how the pedagogy gets — uh — stress-tested. So.

Piotr: Omer.

Omer: Yes.

Piotr: This is the nicest thing anyone has said to me this month.

Omer: It is Tuesday, Piotr.

Piotr: Yes.
